Consists of the type, length, and value, in that order.

The type is one byte in range 0x01 to 0xFF except 0x80, and if it is in the
range 0x78 to 0x7F or 0xF8 to 0xFF then one additional type byte follows.
If the high bit is clear then it is a primitive type, and if the high bit
is set then it is a constructed type.

The length can be one of the following:

* 0x00 to 0xF0 = The value is that many bytes long.

* 0xF1 to 0xF8 = One to eight additional bytes follow, which specify the
length of the value. (Overlong encodings are allowed, since that makes it
possible to fill up a part of the file and then go back and fill up the
length later.)

* 0xF9 = Primitive stream. This is only valid if the type is primitive. In
this case, the value consists of zero or more segments which will be
concatenated together; each segment starts with one byte 0x01 to 0xFF which
specifies the length of that segment (in bytes), and terminated by 0x00.

* 0xFA = Constructed stream. This is only valid if the type is constructed,
and means that the value continues until a byte 0x00 is found at this level
outside of any other primitive or constructed value it contains.

* 0xFB = The rest of the data in this file is this item, so the length is
the remaining length of the file, so further items are not possible. This
is only allowed as the top item in a file or contained directly within a
item having this kind of length, and it limited to only some of the types,
as follows: octet string, ASCII string, mixed string, sequence, structure,
extension, tagged data, and time series. Furthermore, such a thing is not
allowed to contain another one with this kind of length unless the outer
item that contains it is a structure, extension, or tagged data.

* 0xFC to 0xFF = Reserved for future use (possibly never will be used).

The data types are explained below. If the type is primitive, then the
format of the value depends on the type. If the type is constructed, then
the format of the value is zero or more type/length/value items, and the
specific type may include further constraints about what is expected.

[0x01 (Null)] The value is empty.

[0x02 (False)] The value is empty; represents a false boolean value.

[0x03 (True)] The value is empty; represents a true boolean value.

[0x04 (Octet string)] A sequence of bytes.

[0x05 (Bit string)] A sequence of bits, from the high bit to the low bit of
each byte. The last byte of this value tells the number of unused bits in
the low nybble (which must be 0 to 7, so bit3 must be clear); the high
nybble represents four bits of data. Unused bits start from the lowest bits
of the high nybble of the last byte, and the low bits of the previous byte
as well if there is more than four unused bits. Unused bits must be clear.

[0x06 (TRON string)] String of Extended TRON characters, encoded as TRON-8.
Control characters can be included as well and can be used for some kinds
of formatting that do not need other types.

[0x07 (ASCII string)] A string of ASCII characters. It is not supposed to
include characters with the high bit set, but it is allowed and if there
are any then each byte must be interpreted as one character.

[0x08 (Application string)] A string of 8-bit characters, using the
character set specific to the application (which can be declared in
other parts of the file).

[0x09 (Negative integer)] A integer where all of the bits higher than the
explicitly specified bits are implicitly set, making the value negative.

[0x0A (Non-negative integer)] A unsigned integer.

[0x0B (Mixed string)] Like octet string but links and/or capabilities
(which have a length of eight bytes) are also allowed.

[0x0C (Signed object identifier)] An arbitrary nonempty sequence of bytes,
with the high bit of the last byte clear. It is a superset of OIDs, and
have the same binary representation, but invalid OIDs can be valid SOIDs.
See below for more information.

[0x0D (Relative SOID)]

[0x0E (Floating point)]

[0x0F (Multicultural time)]

[0x10 (Name)] A restricted subset of a application string that uses the
CAQL character set, suitable for names in CAQL scripts.

[0x11 (Reference to fork)] Reference to a fork in this or another file.
If this file, it is four bytes specifying the fork number; if another
file, it is the same but also includes the link to the other file (although
this is also possible even if it is the same file).

[0x60 to 0x77 (Context specific)]

[0x7800 to 0x78FF (Context specific)]

[0x81 (Sequence)]

[0x82 (Set)]

[0x83 (Structure)] Contains arbitrary items, interpreted as a structure
with fields. (This is like one of the uses of the "sequence" in ASN.1, the
other use being the type 0x81.)

[0x84 (Extension)]

[0x85 (Key/value list)]

[0x86 (Rational number)] Contains two integers, the first being the
numerator and the second being the denominator.

[0x87 (Complex number)]

[0x88 (Translation list)] This is like a key/value list but the keys
specify the languages, and the corresponding value will be used for
that language.

[0x89 (Section)] A section of a text document. It can contain paragraphs
and other stuff like that, as well as subsections.

[0x8A (Paragraph)]

[0x8B (Text span)]

[0x8C (Block quotation)]

[0x8D (Mathematical typesetting)]

[0x8E (Zoned spreadsheet)]

[0x8F (Calculation)]

[0x90 (Time series)] A sequence of data that is keyed by time intervals,
which are in chronological order.

[0x91 (Tagged data)]

[0x92 (Diagram)]

[0x93 (CAQL script)]

[0x94 (Matrix)]

[0x95 (Function)]

[0x96 (Table)]

[0x97 (Named block)] Assigns a name to a block for the purpose of queries
in this file that refer to blocks by their name and for references to
this part of the file from other files and other parts of this file. It
has two items, the first being the name and the second being the item that
is being named in this way.

[0x98 (Character set declaration)] Declares the character set to be used by
application strings that this block contains.

[0x99 (Alternate representation)] This type is used when the actual data is
stored in another fork of the file in a different format than this one, and
this data item then includes the SOID to specify what format (which another
program might be able to convert, or use directly). For example, this can
be used for compressed files, or for files that might be more efficient in
one way than another way, for B-trees, etc.

[0xE0 to 0xF7 (Context specific)]

[0xF800 to 0xF8FF (Context specific)]


=== Signed object identifiers ===

A signed object identifier (SOID) is like a OID, and is a superset of OIDs,
that allows any nonempty byte sequence, as long as the high bit of the last
byte is clear. Like OIDs, they can be intepreted as a sequence of integers,
and can be made as decimal format; however, the integers can be negative in
addition to positive and zero.

If the first byte of a piece is 0x80 then it represents a negative number.
In this case, treat the rest of the bytes as making up a bijective base 128
number (instead of the non-bijective) and then make it negative; this means
that each byte is worth one more than it normally is.

The first piece is split even if it is negative, and the splitting works as
described in this case: The bitwise complement is used, and then shifted
right by three. If the shifted out bits are 0 to 6 then add 3 to make the
first number, and then the rest of the bits are the second number. If the
shifted out bits are 7 then the first number is 9 and then the rest of the
bits will be complemented (reversing the original complement) and used as
the second number, so that it will be negative.


=== Control characters ===

In some types of character strings, some control characters would be used.

In a TRON string, it might use some of the below codes. The codes <00> to
<20>, <7F>, and codes <FF21> to <FF7E>, can be used as control characters.

<> Begin left to right. (The text direction of the paragraph must be
specified separately (outside of the character strings), and these control
characters are not needed if the text direction matches that of the
paragraph. The control characters to set text direction cannot be used in
vertical paragraphs.)

<> Begin right to left.

<> End left to right.

<> End right to left.

<> Begin base text of furigana block.

<> Begin furigana text of furigana block.

<> End furigana block.

<> Begin bold.

<> End bold.

<> Begin emphasis.

<> End emphasis.

<> Begin underline.

<> End underline.

<> Soft hyphenation.

